{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Perceptron.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Perceptron.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"perceptron perceptron","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Perceptron.md","fileName":"Perceptron.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Perceptron","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-06 00:13\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Artificial Neural Network Structure]] · [[Neural Network Classification and Clustering]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Artificial Neural Network Structure","Neural Network Classification and Clustering","Artificial Neuron","Decision Boundary","Learning Rate","algorithms.epub","clusteranalysisanddatamining.pdf","deeplearning_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Perceptron.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Perceptron.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"perceptron perceptron","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Perceptron.md","fileName":"Perceptron.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Perceptron","heading":"Perceptron","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A Perceptron is an [[Artificial Neuron]] whose activation is a step function. It outputs one when its weighted input and bias cross the threshold and zero otherwise.\n\nA single Perceptron can learn a linear [[Decision Boundary]] and separate two classes when the observations are linearly separable. More complicated boundaries require networks that combine multiple neurons in connected layers.\n\nIn the book's classification treatment, a perceptron has input nodes fully connected to class outputs, combines inputs through weights, and applies an activation function. Its learning rule can find a linear separator when one exists; the XOR example demonstrates why nonlinear problems require hidden layers and a multilayer network.\n\nKelleher describes Rosenblatt's rule as error correction after each labeled example: if the output is correct the weights remain unchanged; otherwise, weights are changed in a direction intended to correct the mistake. The [[Learning Rate]] scales those changes. The convergence result applies when a separating set of weights exists, but an unsuccessful long run alone cannot tell whether convergence is merely slow or impossible for the examples.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Artificial Neural Network Structure","Neural Network Classification and Clustering","Artificial Neuron","Decision Boundary","Learning Rate","algorithms.epub","clusteranalysisanddatamining.pdf","deeplearning_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Perceptron.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Perceptron.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"perceptron perceptron","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Perceptron.md","fileName":"Perceptron.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Perceptron","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[algorithms.epub]]\n\n[[clusteranalysisanddatamining.pdf]]\n\n[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Artificial Neural Network Structure","Neural Network Classification and Clustering","Artificial Neuron","Decision Boundary","Learning Rate","algorithms.epub","clusteranalysisanddatamining.pdf","deeplearning_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}